W: 1. It was Yankee the Squire as I’ve heard them tell
W: He went out a-hunting all on one fine day
W: He went out a-hunting but nothing he found
W: But a poor murdered woman laid on the cold ground.
W:
W: 2. About eight o'clock, boys, our dogs they throwed off
W: And off to the Common and that was the spot
W: They tried all the bushes but nothing they found
W: But a poor murdered woman laid on the cold ground.
W:
W: 3. They whipped their dogs off and they kept them away
W: For I do think it is proper that she should have fair play
W: They tried all the bushes but nothing they found
W: But a poor murdered woman laid on the cold ground.
W:
W: 4. They mounted their horses and they rode off the ground
W: They rode to the village and alarmed it all around
W: It is late in the evening, I am sorry to say,
W: She cannot be removed until the net day."
W:
W: 5. The net Sunday morning about eight o’clock
W: Some hundreds of people to the spot they did flock
W: For to see that poor creature it would make your hearts bleed
W: Some cold-hearted violence came into their heads.
W:
W: 6. She was took off the Common and down to some inn
W: And the man that has kept it his name is John Simms
W: The Coroner was sent for and the jury they joined
W: And soon they concluded and they settled their mind.
W:
W: 7. A coffin was brought and in it she was laid
W: And took to the churchyard in fair Leatherhead
W: No father, no mother, nor no friend at all
W: Came to see the poor creature put under the mould.
%
